1
00:00:13,930 --> 00:00:16,724
Lisbon. Lisbon!

2
00:00:23,147 --> 00:00:24,232
Don't move.

3
00:00:26,776 --> 00:00:27,818
Does it bite?

4
00:00:33,324 --> 00:00:34,367
He could've killed me.

5
00:00:43,918 --> 00:00:46,671
- CBI?
- Yes, Sir. Lisbon. Jane.

6
00:00:46,921 --> 00:00:48,506
Mullery. What took you so long?

7
00:00:49,131 --> 00:00:51,884
Sheriff, I understand this
is a tough situation for you.

8
00:00:52,718 --> 00:00:53,844
Tough situation?

9
00:00:54,595 --> 00:00:58,516
This is deputy Nelson Capel.
Been with me 15 years. Got 3 kids.

10
00:00:59,183 --> 00:01:02,353
He answered a 9-1-1 call last night
at 10:23 from a local girl

11
00:01:02,478 --> 00:01:04,438
Keeley Farlowe, on her cell phone.

12
00:01:04,897 --> 00:01:07,191
She gave her location here,
and the line went dead.

13
00:01:10,027 --> 00:01:12,572
This is deputy Lance Connor.
Five years in.

14
00:01:14,031 --> 00:01:15,825
His dad was in high school with me.

15
00:01:17,660 --> 00:01:20,997
The E. M. T. captain you see
there crying like a baby

16
00:01:21,372 --> 00:01:22,748
that's my nephew Todd.

17
00:01:23,791 --> 00:01:28,129
The reason he's crying, Keeley,
that's his fiancée, throat cut.

18
00:01:28,296 --> 00:01:30,506
So, yeah, tough situation.

19
00:01:30,631 --> 00:01:32,800
You go ahead and look at whatever
it is you wanna look at.

20
00:01:33,175 --> 00:01:35,761
Let me know when you're done, so we
can take these folks someplace decent.

21
00:01:37,930 --> 00:01:39,473
Yeesh. This is trouble.

22
00:01:40,766 --> 00:01:41,892
Interesting, though.

23
00:01:42,268 --> 00:01:45,771
Three different methods of killing.
Why is that?

24
00:01:45,896 --> 00:01:49,233
It's always bad when local cops
are personally involved.

25
00:01:49,567 --> 00:01:50,860
Think the feds would want this one?

26
00:01:51,027 --> 00:01:53,779
The bodies have been arranged as
well, not in an ostentatious way

27
00:01:53,904 --> 00:01:57,491
but there's a definite
aesthetic at work here.

28
00:01:57,617 --> 00:01:58,868
He took his time.

29
00:01:59,702 --> 00:02:03,039
Yeah, it's a truly complicated maniac
we're dealing with.

30
00:02:03,331 --> 00:02:07,209
- Yes. I think I will call the feds.
- No, don't do that.

31
00:02:07,668 --> 00:02:10,963
I don't know why,
but I... I want this case.

32
00:02:12,089 --> 00:02:13,132
Or vice versa.

33
00:02:15,009 --> 00:02:17,303
All right, but I have a
feeling I'm gonna be sorry.

34
00:02:17,803 --> 00:02:19,055
You people done yet?

35
00:02:19,889 --> 00:02:22,099
Sheriff Mullery, just so
we're on the same page

36
00:02:22,266 --> 00:02:25,728
in cases like these, we,
the CBI, have sole jurisdiction.

37
00:02:25,895 --> 00:02:28,105
Your department would have
a strictly advisory role.

38
00:02:28,898 --> 00:02:31,067
- That's the way...
- You can have all the jurisdiction you want.

39
00:02:31,192 --> 00:02:34,570
I swear to God, the man who did this
will not live to see a courtroom.

40
00:02:37,365 --> 00:02:38,407
You didn't hear that.

41
00:02:42,870 --> 00:02:45,081
Yep. I'm already sorry.

42
00:03:18,404 --> 00:03:20,447
{\an8}- What's the password for wi-fi?
- Say?

43
00:03:21,115 --> 00:03:22,324
1-2-3-4.

44
00:03:23,867 --> 00:03:25,077
Of course.

45
00:03:25,744 --> 00:03:27,996
{\an8}So... CBI.

46
00:03:28,706 --> 00:03:29,748
Top guns.

47
00:03:30,165 --> 00:03:33,043
{\an8}- Not really. I'm agent Grace Van Pelt.
- Bob Woolgar.

48
00:03:33,502 --> 00:03:35,921
{\an8}Number two around here.
That's Hobbs.

49
00:03:37,506 --> 00:03:38,674
{\an8}Sir, may I help you?

50
00:03:39,007 --> 00:03:42,219
{\an8}May I please speak with whoever is
in charge of the murder investigation?

51
00:03:42,344 --> 00:03:45,723
{\an8}- Why is that?
- I'm sorry. Are you in charge?

52
00:03:46,890 --> 00:03:48,767
- No, but I...
- I thought not.

53
00:03:49,101 --> 00:03:52,271
{\an8}Sir, I'm with the CBI.
We're in charge of this investigation.

54
00:03:52,479 --> 00:03:54,732
{\an8}If you have any information about the
case, I'd be more than happy to help.

55
00:03:54,857 --> 00:03:55,899
{\an8}Miss, no offense.

56
00:03:56,024 --> 00:03:58,944
{\an8}You are far too young and pretty
to have any authority in the matter.

57
00:03:59,069 --> 00:04:01,739
{\an8}I shall wait for your chief.

58
00:04:13,751 --> 00:04:16,044
{\an8}"If you're media, keep movin'."

59
00:04:19,131 --> 00:04:20,466
{\an8}CBI. Police.

60
00:04:22,259 --> 00:04:23,927
{\an8}Put the weapon down right now.

61
00:04:25,637 --> 00:04:26,930
All right. No problem.

62
00:04:28,307 --> 00:04:29,475
No need to shout.

63
00:04:33,604 --> 00:04:35,814
{\an8}- And you are...
- I'm Joe Hunnicker.

64
00:04:37,357 --> 00:04:40,110
{\an8}Keeley's my granddaughter.
I live here with her and Todd.

65
00:04:42,070 --> 00:04:43,113
Come on in.

66
00:04:49,036 --> 00:04:50,329
{\an8}She was a good girl.

67
00:04:51,747 --> 00:04:53,540
You know, if she and Todd
hadn't brought me in here

68
00:04:53,665 --> 00:04:56,585
{\an8}on my V.A. check, I'd be
in a hostel somewhere.

69
00:04:57,169 --> 00:04:58,212
{\an8}Hey, Todd.

70
00:04:59,213 --> 00:05:00,714
Lisbon. Jane.

71
00:05:02,758 --> 00:05:04,968
Oh hey. Yes, we met.

72
00:05:06,386 --> 00:05:08,639
{\an8}Really, I-I apologize
for my outburst there.

73
00:05:08,764 --> 00:05:12,309
I was, uh, a little crazy.
You can imagine.

74
00:05:13,018 --> 00:05:14,520
Calmed down a little now.

75
00:05:15,646 --> 00:05:18,607
Rest assured, I-I won't be
pulling any vigilante nonsense.

76
00:05:18,774 --> 00:05:20,275
We're very sorry for your loss.

77
00:05:21,068 --> 00:05:22,444
And I'm glad you see it that way.

78
00:05:22,778 --> 00:05:25,113
There's no upside to taking
the law into your own hands.

79
00:05:25,322 --> 00:05:26,573
Revenge truly doesn't work.

80
00:05:26,698 --> 00:05:29,368
Well, that's just a sweeping
statement, isn't it?

81
00:05:30,118 --> 00:05:32,996
I'm sure we can all think of examples
where revenge has worked splendidly.

82
00:05:33,247 --> 00:05:34,289
Thanks for your input.

83
00:05:34,832 --> 00:05:35,874
Just sayin'.

84
00:05:38,460 --> 00:05:39,503
Tell us about Keeley.

85
00:05:41,964 --> 00:05:43,006
I don't know what to say.

86
00:05:44,550 --> 00:05:45,592
I love her.

87
00:05:46,385 --> 00:05:47,427
We're getting married.

88
00:05:51,557 --> 00:05:53,225
- We were.
- Parents?

89
00:05:53,392 --> 00:05:56,061
Auto accident about five years ago.

90
00:05:56,895 --> 00:05:59,022
Drunk driver.
Both dead.

91
00:05:59,690 --> 00:06:01,275
When was the last time
you spoke to her?

92
00:06:02,192 --> 00:06:07,823
About an hour before she was
due home... I called her and I...

93
00:06:08,115 --> 00:06:11,076
I asked her to bring
home some beer and chips.

94
00:06:11,201 --> 00:06:13,579
Me and Hunny were...
were playin' Gin Rummy.

95
00:06:13,996 --> 00:06:15,372
And I could've picked her up.

96
00:06:16,790 --> 00:06:19,084
You know, I often do when
it's raining or whatever

97
00:06:19,209 --> 00:06:22,963
but last night... Last night I
was just... I was lazy, I guess.

98
00:06:23,964 --> 00:06:26,008
It's basically my fault that she's dead.

99
00:06:27,968 --> 00:06:30,679
Don't. Don't say that, son.

100
00:06:31,763 --> 00:06:33,098
Don't take that on yourself.

101
00:06:36,894 --> 00:06:38,228
Please find who did this.

102
00:06:40,105 --> 00:06:41,648
I just wanna know why.

103
00:06:44,359 --> 00:06:45,402
Why?

104
00:06:50,073 --> 00:06:51,950
Keeley worked the late shift
for the last year.

105
00:06:52,492 --> 00:06:54,286
4:00 to 10:00, as reliable as clockwork.

106
00:06:54,828 --> 00:06:56,622
Great kid. Everybody loved her.

107
00:06:56,914 --> 00:06:59,833
- Always had a smile for you, you know?
- Yeah, she thought the best of folks.

108
00:07:00,542 --> 00:07:03,003
- Kinda naive that way.
- Meaning anyone in particular?

109
00:07:03,962 --> 00:07:06,548
No, just, you know, in general, trusting.

110
00:07:06,924 --> 00:07:08,592
Last night, what time
did she leave work?

111
00:07:09,051 --> 00:07:12,596
10:00, as always,
on that so-and-so bicycle.

112
00:07:13,096 --> 00:07:16,266
I told her it wasn't safe, but she said
it saved money and kept her skinny.

113
00:07:16,558 --> 00:07:19,061
Did anything unusual happen
during her shift? Anything at all?

114
00:07:20,145 --> 00:07:21,188
Not that I saw.

115
00:07:23,357 --> 00:07:25,233
There was this trucker
tried to pick her up.

116
00:07:25,400 --> 00:07:26,777
Come in a few times now.

117
00:07:27,235 --> 00:07:29,029
Always left her, like, a 10-buck tip.

118
00:07:29,738 --> 00:07:32,574
He was in here yesterday around closing
time. You remember him, Rusty?

119
00:07:33,533 --> 00:07:36,328
She used to love to show her engagement
ring to guys to shut 'em down

120
00:07:36,703 --> 00:07:38,789
but this guy was like, "I don't care."
He kept on trying.

121
00:07:38,914 --> 00:07:41,333
- Did you get his name?
- It says "Greg" on his shirt.

122
00:07:42,334 --> 00:07:44,169
You remember him
big, old, greasy fella.

123
00:07:45,796 --> 00:07:47,381
Nope. Can't say I do.

124
00:07:48,131 --> 00:07:51,551
He drives this truck with this
big smiling orange on the side

125
00:07:52,052 --> 00:07:55,597
with arms, like, "Hi. I'm an orange."

126
00:08:00,394 --> 00:08:02,896
"Examples where revenge
worked splendidly."

127
00:08:03,105 --> 00:08:05,232
- Nice.
- I was just teasing.

128
00:08:05,482 --> 00:08:07,859
A little hypocritical of you to lecture him
on revenge, don't you think?

129
00:08:08,026 --> 00:08:09,069
How's that?

130
00:08:09,653 --> 00:08:12,072
All these years you've
been helping me seek revenge?

131
00:08:12,990 --> 00:08:14,032
That's different.

132
00:08:15,283 --> 00:08:17,703
- How's that?
- I'm in control of the situation.

133
00:08:18,161 --> 00:08:19,204
Oh, really?

134
00:08:22,958 --> 00:08:25,627
She must have been abducted
somewhere along this stretch of road.

135
00:08:26,503 --> 00:08:27,963
Strange we haven't found her bike.

136
00:08:28,964 --> 00:08:30,632
Maybe she didn't head straight home.

137
00:08:33,010 --> 00:08:36,096
What do you mean, "You're in
control of the situation"?

138
00:08:37,305 --> 00:08:39,599
When the day comes,
when we find Red John

139
00:08:39,725 --> 00:08:42,227
I'm gonna be there to stop you
from doing anything foolish.

140
00:08:42,352 --> 00:08:44,396
We're gonna put him in
front of a judge and a jury.

141
00:08:44,563 --> 00:08:45,897
- You really believe that?
- Yes, I do.

142
00:08:46,106 --> 00:08:47,149
Really?

143
00:08:48,900 --> 00:08:51,570
I just assumed you took that judge
and jury stuff with a grain of salt.

144
00:08:51,695 --> 00:08:53,697
Well, you assumed wrong. No salt.

145
00:08:57,367 --> 00:08:59,411
You know the reality will be different.

146
00:09:00,412 --> 00:09:04,124
Red John is mine, and I'm gonna exact
my revenge on him.

147
00:09:04,750 --> 00:09:08,462
I think, when the time comes,
you'll see that violence is not the answer.

148
00:09:14,009 --> 00:09:17,637
I had no idea your naive idealism
was so deeply felt.

149
00:09:17,763 --> 00:09:19,973
- Hush.
- Hush.

150
00:09:20,265 --> 00:09:21,308
Hush.

151
00:09:22,851 --> 00:09:25,437
Excuse me.
You are agent Teresa Lisbon?

152
00:09:25,771 --> 00:09:27,439
- Yes, I am.
- Good evening, ma'am.

153
00:09:27,981 --> 00:09:29,608
My name is Ellis Mars.

154
00:09:30,400 --> 00:09:33,445
I understand you are leading the
Keeley Farlowe murder investigation.

155
00:09:33,820 --> 00:09:34,863
Yes.

156
00:09:36,031 --> 00:09:37,074
Poor girl.

157
00:09:37,240 --> 00:09:40,702
I often saw her around
town, so fresh, innocent.

158
00:09:41,286 --> 00:09:42,329
Yes?

159
00:09:44,706 --> 00:09:45,791
I have information.

160
00:09:46,792 --> 00:09:49,586
The killer is going to strike again.
Soon.

161
00:09:50,003 --> 00:09:51,338
And how do you know that, Sir?

162
00:09:52,631 --> 00:09:53,673
Well, it's hard to explain.

163
00:09:53,799 --> 00:09:57,677
Some call it a third eye or E.S.P.
I think of it simply as my gift.

164
00:10:00,222 --> 00:10:01,556
Not interested. Thanks, though.

165
00:10:01,681 --> 00:10:03,100
Well, I've been waiting here
for several hours.

166
00:10:04,142 --> 00:10:05,644
Well, perhaps you are
more broad-minded, Sir.

167
00:10:05,769 --> 00:10:08,313
Uh, don't get me started.

168
00:10:08,772 --> 00:10:12,109
I forgive your brusqueness. I can tell that
you have been visited by great tragedy.

169
00:10:16,863 --> 00:10:17,906
Oh, really?

170
00:10:18,782 --> 00:10:22,536
I'm sorry. I did not mean to offend.
I just, uh, saw it in your eyes.

171
00:10:24,162 --> 00:10:25,205
Good-bye, Mr. Mars.

172
00:10:25,455 --> 00:10:27,582
If you do need my help
again, please call me.

173
00:10:27,791 --> 00:10:28,959
I'll happily come back.

174
00:10:33,130 --> 00:10:36,049
Cho and Rigsby have gone back to
Sacramento to pick up Gregor Hobart

175
00:10:36,258 --> 00:10:38,051
drives a truck for Happy Citrus Farms.

176
00:10:38,260 --> 00:10:39,928
Was seen talking to Keeley
as she left work.

177
00:10:40,053 --> 00:10:41,096
What about forensics?

178
00:10:41,221 --> 00:10:42,973
They put a rush on the report
be here first thing in the morning

179
00:10:43,098 --> 00:10:45,433
and I booked us rooms
at the nearest motel.

180
00:10:46,059 --> 00:10:47,978
Reasonable price.
Deputy Hobbs says it's clean.

181
00:10:48,311 --> 00:10:49,855
Good. It's been a long day.

182
00:10:52,023 --> 00:10:53,066
You all right, Jane?

183
00:10:53,191 --> 00:10:55,777
Oh, small-town motel, gas station
pizza. What could be better?

184
00:11:07,330 --> 00:11:08,915
"Tyger, tyger."

185
00:11:09,457 --> 00:11:10,709
"Tyger, tyger, burning bright."

186
00:11:10,834 --> 00:11:14,337
"Tyger, tyger
In the forests of the night"

187
00:11:16,131 --> 00:11:17,424
"What immortal hand or eye"

188
00:11:17,549 --> 00:11:19,301
"Could frame thy fearful symmetry?"

189
00:11:20,093 --> 00:11:21,136
"Symmetry?"

190
00:11:28,268 --> 00:11:29,352
"Gregor Hobart?"

191
00:11:30,312 --> 00:11:31,980
- Yes.
- Don't run.

192
00:11:33,565 --> 00:11:35,775
- Wasn't going to.
- Yeah, you had that look about you.

193
00:11:35,984 --> 00:11:37,402
No one calls me Gregor.

194
00:11:39,446 --> 00:11:41,156
- What's this about?
- Keeley Farlowe.

195
00:11:41,281 --> 00:11:42,490
She's dead, murdered.

196
00:11:45,410 --> 00:11:46,453
Oh, no.

197
00:11:46,578 --> 00:11:49,497
- You were fond of Keeley, were you?
- Yes, I was.

198
00:11:50,999 --> 00:11:52,918
- Did you ever tell her that?
- Sure.

199
00:11:53,710 --> 00:11:56,213
- I asked her out.
- What was her response?

200
00:11:57,672 --> 00:11:59,633
Uh, I think she was intrigued.

201
00:12:00,675 --> 00:12:01,968
She was engaged to be married.

202
00:12:02,677 --> 00:12:06,264
We're all sinners, brother,
even nice girls like Keeley.

203
00:12:06,598 --> 00:12:09,851
- And when did you last speak to her?
- Yesterday, uh, when she left work.

204
00:12:10,143 --> 00:12:11,561
When she left work? Where?

205
00:12:12,896 --> 00:12:15,857
Outside the diner, in the street.
She was unlocking her bike.

206
00:12:15,982 --> 00:12:17,817
That makes you the last person
to see her alive.

207
00:12:18,568 --> 00:12:20,612
- Apart from her killer.
- Right. So you said hi?

208
00:12:21,404 --> 00:12:22,989
I said "hi." She said "hi."

209
00:12:23,573 --> 00:12:24,699
She was upset

210
00:12:25,951 --> 00:12:27,452
and so I tried to make her feel better.

211
00:12:28,203 --> 00:12:29,371
I told her a joke.

212
00:12:29,913 --> 00:12:33,166
- This goat walks into a bar...
- What was she upset about?

213
00:12:34,084 --> 00:12:37,003
The cook at the dinner was hassling
her on the sly, grabbing at her.

214
00:12:37,128 --> 00:12:39,047
You know, a secret lech.

215
00:12:39,965 --> 00:12:41,883
They had a tussle just before I saw her.

216
00:12:42,717 --> 00:12:44,010
So she was upset.

217
00:12:46,221 --> 00:12:49,891
So anyway, I made her laugh
she rode off happy.

218
00:12:50,141 --> 00:12:51,393
And that's the last you saw of her?

219
00:12:52,102 --> 00:12:53,144
Yeah.

220
00:12:53,853 --> 00:12:54,896
The strange thing?

221
00:12:57,691 --> 00:13:02,612
When she was leaving, the moon
was coming up over the hills

222
00:13:04,072 --> 00:13:09,119
and it looked like she was
riding to the moon... You know?

223
00:13:10,787 --> 00:13:13,331
I was admiring how pretty she looked.

224
00:13:14,833 --> 00:13:18,962
And at the same time,
it gave me a bad feeling

225
00:13:21,131 --> 00:13:22,424
like an evil omen.

226
00:13:22,841 --> 00:13:26,428
We hear you were harassing Keeley
Farlowe, putting your hands on her?

227
00:13:27,595 --> 00:13:28,638
No.

228
00:13:28,763 --> 00:13:30,265
We hear you had a fight with her
yesterday.

229
00:13:30,640 --> 00:13:31,683
No.

230
00:13:35,645 --> 00:13:39,190
- It's hot in here. Roll up your sleeves.
- That's okay. I'm not hot.

231
00:13:49,200 --> 00:13:50,452
Look, a cat did this.

232
00:13:57,584 --> 00:13:59,210
There is no cat, Rusty

233
00:13:59,544 --> 00:14:02,964
and forensics will find your skin under
Keeley's fingernails, won't they?

234
00:14:06,426 --> 00:14:07,469
I want a lawyer.

235
00:14:07,969 --> 00:14:10,597
Leave him alone with me for ten minutes.
I'll get a confession.

236
00:14:10,847 --> 00:14:11,890
That's a great idea.

237
00:14:12,932 --> 00:14:14,351
That's not the way we work.

238
00:14:14,684 --> 00:14:17,520
Don't worry about it, little lady.
Go yourself a latte.

239
00:14:17,937 --> 00:14:19,606
Check your e-mail.
It won't take long.

240
00:14:20,231 --> 00:14:22,817
Sheriff, I believe we have
some cultural differences here.

241
00:14:22,942 --> 00:14:24,861
I think we'll take this gentleman
back to Sacramento

242
00:14:24,986 --> 00:14:27,072
question him there, avoid friction.

243
00:14:27,697 --> 00:14:28,740
Come on.

244
00:14:33,078 --> 00:14:34,454
You had her at "little lady."

245
00:14:35,330 --> 00:14:38,375
My aunt had a cat.
Little Charly Manson with fur.

246
00:14:38,583 --> 00:14:40,293
"Percy" he was called.
Get it?

247
00:14:40,710 --> 00:14:43,088
Purr... See. Interesting anecdote.

248
00:14:43,421 --> 00:14:45,590
Just trying to keep our
friend at ease, right?

249
00:14:52,430 --> 00:14:54,057
- Hey! Hey!
- I'll kill you!

250
00:15:00,688 --> 00:15:01,731
Relax, Sir.

251
00:15:01,856 --> 00:15:04,401
Relax and the pain will stop.

252
00:15:11,072 --> 00:15:13,825
Tell me why I shouldn't charge
Todd Johnson with assault.

253
00:15:13,950 --> 00:15:16,035
- I think you should.
- But he's a good kid.

254
00:15:16,661 --> 00:15:17,703
He just got carried away.

255
00:15:17,829 --> 00:15:20,289
Are we gonna ruin his life
with a criminal record?

256
00:15:20,415 --> 00:15:22,125
I mean, hasn't he
suffered enough already?

257
00:15:22,667 --> 00:15:25,545
Vengeance is not legal.
People need to know that.

258
00:15:26,754 --> 00:15:30,049
So I have justice and humanity on my side
and Lisbon has a teachable moment.

259
00:15:30,383 --> 00:15:31,426
Let me talk to him.

260
00:15:31,551 --> 00:15:33,678
I will persuade him to drop
the notion of revenge.

261
00:15:33,845 --> 00:15:36,305
- I'll put him back on the right path.
- You think you can do that?

262
00:15:37,432 --> 00:15:38,474
I know I can.

263
00:15:39,142 --> 00:15:40,184
I've been there.

264
00:15:44,689 --> 00:15:45,815
Okay, go do that.

265
00:15:47,442 --> 00:15:52,363
And if he says the right things, and I
believe him, then he can walk away.

266
00:15:52,488 --> 00:15:53,531
Yes, ma'am.

267
00:16:03,583 --> 00:16:04,625
Have a seat.

268
00:16:09,005 --> 00:16:10,631
You just tried to kill an innocent man.

269
00:16:11,215 --> 00:16:13,092
- You're very lucky you failed.
- I didn't fail.

270
00:16:14,510 --> 00:16:17,013
I mean, I wasn't thinking.

271
00:16:17,722 --> 00:16:19,015
I don't know what I was doing.

272
00:16:21,350 --> 00:16:24,145
- You're sure he's not the guy?
- He's not the guy. Trust me.

273
00:16:25,480 --> 00:16:27,940
CBI chief wants to charge you
with assault.

274
00:16:28,191 --> 00:16:32,028
- Well, I guess I got it coming to me.
- Unless... Unless...

275
00:16:33,446 --> 00:16:35,406
You're willing to swear
you've learned your lesson

276
00:16:35,615 --> 00:16:37,700
and you won't pursue
personal vengeance.

277
00:16:40,077 --> 00:16:41,496
I can't do that, Mr. Jane.

278
00:16:43,956 --> 00:16:45,249
I find who did this to...

279
00:16:46,667 --> 00:16:48,503
To Keeley, I will kill him.

280
00:16:49,962 --> 00:16:51,422
I can't swear to do otherwise.

281
00:16:59,347 --> 00:17:01,557
Todd, you repeat what I tell you now

282
00:17:03,017 --> 00:17:04,727
and I will deny every word of it.

283
00:17:07,188 --> 00:17:10,024
If you truly want revenge,
you have to be hard.

284
00:17:10,733 --> 00:17:14,612
You have to be dishonest
and devious and cold.

285
00:17:17,949 --> 00:17:20,201
You can't let people see
what's in your heart.

286
00:17:27,166 --> 00:17:28,209
Okay.

287
00:17:31,462 --> 00:17:36,133
So anyhow, I'm-I'm very sorry.
I swear it will never happen again.

288
00:17:40,555 --> 00:17:41,889
I'm glad to hear you say that.

289
00:17:43,140 --> 00:17:45,601
And I'm glad to say that you
can walk out of here a free man.

290
00:17:46,352 --> 00:17:47,395
No charges.

291
00:17:48,145 --> 00:17:49,188
Thank you.

292
00:17:54,443 --> 00:17:55,486
Thank you.

293
00:17:58,656 --> 00:18:01,909
- That was all B.S., right?
- It sounded pretty sincere to me.

294
00:18:02,201 --> 00:18:04,954
- I hope you know what you're doing.
- About 78% sure.

295
00:18:06,080 --> 00:18:08,499
- Grace, what is it?
- The ballistics report came in.

296
00:18:08,666 --> 00:18:11,419
Deputy Connor was shot
with the same 45 caliber handgun

297
00:18:11,544 --> 00:18:13,045
that was used
in the Howard-Ortiz murders.

298
00:18:13,170 --> 00:18:16,215
- Oh, crap.
- Howard-Ortiz murders?

299
00:18:17,508 --> 00:18:19,885
Two months ago, sac P.D. officer
Jeff Ortiz

300
00:18:20,052 --> 00:18:21,762
was shot dead by a passing motorist.

301
00:18:22,054 --> 00:18:25,474
Then exactly one month later, C.H.P.
officer Rance Howard

302
00:18:25,600 --> 00:18:27,143
was shot dead
with the same handgun.

303
00:18:27,518 --> 00:18:29,729
Both incidents occurred late at night
on country roads.

304
00:18:30,229 --> 00:18:32,523
Howard used to work here
before your time. Good guy.

305
00:18:32,815 --> 00:18:34,859
The San Francisco office has the case.
Did you talk to 'em?

306
00:18:35,026 --> 00:18:36,861
Yeah, they're sending over
a copy of the case files.

307
00:18:37,361 --> 00:18:40,573
A serial killer of policemen.
Very interesting.

308
00:18:40,823 --> 00:18:43,868
This is not a fascinating puzzle.
It's a media crap storm.

309
00:18:45,202 --> 00:18:46,245
Okay.

310
00:18:47,997 --> 00:18:51,000
San Francisco basically has nothing
on the Howard-Ortiz murders.

311
00:18:51,417 --> 00:18:54,837
No forensics apart from ballistics
no witnesses, no leads.

312
00:18:55,421 --> 00:18:58,215
And Rusty more was on vacation in
Florida when the first murder occurred.

313
00:18:59,342 --> 00:19:01,802
Hobart's company log puts him
out of range in both instances.

314
00:19:01,927 --> 00:19:03,971
So we can discard both
of them as suspects.

315
00:19:04,388 --> 00:19:07,892
Well, forensics found no suspicious
D.N.A. on Keeley or the two deputies.

316
00:19:08,142 --> 00:19:10,436
The tire tracks at the scene
of the crime were standard make.

317
00:19:10,686 --> 00:19:12,563
Over 5 million sold in California alone.

318
00:19:13,814 --> 00:19:14,940
We got nothing, basically.

319
00:19:15,149 --> 00:19:18,694
Well, except, a monthly pattern,
a. 45 caliber handgun

320
00:19:18,861 --> 00:19:20,237
and a grudge against the police.

321
00:19:20,488 --> 00:19:23,324
- But why Keeley? She's not police.
- Why not Keeley?

322
00:19:24,700 --> 00:19:25,868
He sees her riding along.

323
00:19:26,369 --> 00:19:27,745
It's easy to knock her off her bike.

324
00:19:27,953 --> 00:19:30,665
Now he has bait
to lure the deputies into a trap.

325
00:19:30,873 --> 00:19:33,209
In any case, the killer's
gotten more elaborate.

326
00:19:33,334 --> 00:19:34,669
It started with a simple drive-by.

327
00:19:34,794 --> 00:19:37,672
Now we have multiple victims
staging of bodies, arson.

328
00:19:37,797 --> 00:19:40,716
He's beginning to enjoy himself
spreading his wings.

329
00:19:41,342 --> 00:19:44,512
I'm reading your interview
with Hobart, the poetical trucker.

330
00:19:45,721 --> 00:19:48,182
He says that when Keeley
rode off, and I quote

331
00:19:49,100 --> 00:19:50,768
"The moon was coming up
over the hills"

332
00:19:50,893 --> 00:19:53,646
"and it looked like she was
riding to the moon, you know?"

333
00:19:53,896 --> 00:19:55,815
"And I was admiring
how pretty she looked"

334
00:19:56,232 --> 00:20:01,112
"and at the same time, I had a bad
feeling about it, like an evil omen."

335
00:20:05,449 --> 00:20:07,993
- Moon rises in the east.
- So she was riding east.

336
00:20:08,244 --> 00:20:09,954
But her route home is west of the diner.

337
00:20:10,246 --> 00:20:11,956
She wasn't headed home
when she left work.

338
00:20:12,081 --> 00:20:14,709
We've been looking for the abduction
site on the wrong stretch of road.

339
00:20:27,888 --> 00:20:30,015
Yeah, that's Keeley's bike.

340
00:20:31,684 --> 00:20:34,562
Okay. We're gonna have to
cordon off this whole area.

341
00:20:34,812 --> 00:20:36,731
I'm gonna call forensics,
get some techs down here.

342
00:20:38,149 --> 00:20:39,859
Where does this go?
Where-where was she headed?

343
00:20:40,776 --> 00:20:44,488
About half a mile down, there's a
make-out spot that the local kids use.

344
00:20:44,613 --> 00:20:47,992
After that, nothing till you get
to Gainersville, 20 miles away.

345
00:20:48,909 --> 00:20:50,828
Hey, I got somethin'.

346
00:20:52,663 --> 00:20:55,750
Looks like a button from a peacoat
type of thing, like sailors wear?

347
00:20:56,083 --> 00:20:58,294
- Sailors and, uh, firemen.
- Firemen.

348
00:21:01,630 --> 00:21:03,048
We're investigating
the orchard murders.

349
00:21:03,174 --> 00:21:05,926
We need a list of all the firefighters
that weren't on duty the night before last

350
00:21:06,510 --> 00:21:08,554
and anyone who was supposed to
be on duty but didn't show up.

351
00:21:09,597 --> 00:21:10,639
Okay.

352
00:21:11,515 --> 00:21:13,517
- You look troubled.
- No.

353
00:21:14,727 --> 00:21:17,980
Yeah, actually, I mean, there was one guy
who didn't show up that night, but...

354
00:21:18,689 --> 00:21:20,524
There's no way. He's-he's
not that kind of guy.

355
00:21:20,649 --> 00:21:21,817
What's his name?

356
00:21:23,110 --> 00:21:24,153
Kitchen.

357
00:21:24,570 --> 00:21:26,572
- Ben Kitchen.
- And where can we find Mr. Kitchen?

358
00:21:30,493 --> 00:21:32,119
Yo, Kitch. Get out here.

359
00:21:36,957 --> 00:21:38,626
These detectives wanna talk to you.

360
00:21:44,006 --> 00:21:47,259
- Okay. What'd I do?
- How'd you lose your eyebrows?

361
00:21:47,885 --> 00:21:50,095
Barbecue accident, at home.

362
00:21:51,055 --> 00:21:52,723
And why didn't you come into work
the night before last?

363
00:21:53,682 --> 00:21:54,725
Stomach bug.

364
00:21:55,559 --> 00:21:56,894
Might be it was the barbecue.

365
00:22:00,105 --> 00:22:02,691
- What's this about?
- Your lack of eyebrows.

366
00:22:05,402 --> 00:22:06,529
I know what she looks like.

367
00:22:07,363 --> 00:22:08,405
I didn't kill her.

368
00:22:09,156 --> 00:22:12,535
I was home, like I said
a million times now.

369
00:22:13,327 --> 00:22:14,745
You say that, but you have no proof.

370
00:22:14,995 --> 00:22:16,747
I don't need proof. You need proof.

371
00:22:18,123 --> 00:22:19,333
You have a bad attitude.

372
00:22:20,709 --> 00:22:23,003
- What's the matter?
- My face hurts.

373
00:22:23,671 --> 00:22:24,713
You don't like policemen?

374
00:22:26,799 --> 00:22:27,842
Don't love 'em.

375
00:22:29,760 --> 00:22:30,928
It's nothing personal.

376
00:22:31,262 --> 00:22:33,889
It's a firefighter's tradition.

377
00:22:37,309 --> 00:22:38,352
Sheriff Mullery.

378
00:22:38,519 --> 00:22:41,230
Hey, Lisbon, I hear you
got a suspect in custody.

379
00:22:41,522 --> 00:22:43,065
Yeah. He looks pretty good for it.

380
00:22:44,191 --> 00:22:46,402
- Firefighter, huh?
- Yeah. Ben Kitchen?

381
00:22:46,902 --> 00:22:49,989
Yeah, I know him. All right.
Look, I'm on my way to the office now.

382
00:22:50,155 --> 00:22:52,533
That's all right, Sheriff.
It's early days yet.

383
00:22:52,700 --> 00:22:55,202
- "We don't even know if he..."
- Don't worry. I won't interfere.

384
00:22:55,578 --> 00:22:56,829
I learned my lesson...

385
00:22:59,832 --> 00:23:02,459
"Hello? Sheriff?"

386
00:23:04,545 --> 00:23:06,005
"Sheriff Mullery? Hello?"

387
00:23:08,382 --> 00:23:09,425
"Hello?"

388
00:23:38,535 --> 00:23:39,912
Waiting for confirmation
from ballistics

389
00:23:40,078 --> 00:23:42,623
but the M.E. took what looks
like a 45 slug from Mullery.

390
00:23:42,998 --> 00:23:45,417
Same caliber as the previous
murders, same M.O.

391
00:23:45,709 --> 00:23:48,879
So we have to assume it's the same
shooter, only a different schedule.

392
00:23:49,129 --> 00:23:51,173
Three days from the last murder
instead of a month.

393
00:23:51,423 --> 00:23:54,009
- Why the change?
- Because he had to kill Mullery.

394
00:23:54,676 --> 00:23:56,720
Because Mullery
discovered something.

395
00:23:56,845 --> 00:23:58,555
Why did Mullery keep it quiet then?

396
00:23:59,514 --> 00:24:01,975
And how did the killer know
that he had discovered something?

397
00:24:02,768 --> 00:24:03,852
Good questions.

398
00:24:04,227 --> 00:24:06,647
Sir, I don't care what you have
to say, you're not allowed in.

399
00:24:06,813 --> 00:24:08,398
Please, deputy. I am trying to help.

400
00:24:08,565 --> 00:24:10,108
Hobbs, it's okay. I'll deal with him.

401
00:24:11,568 --> 00:24:13,278
What brings you back here, Mr. Mars?

402
00:24:13,654 --> 00:24:15,489
I'm sorry. I never caught your name.

403
00:24:15,614 --> 00:24:17,324
Patrick... Jane.

404
00:24:17,699 --> 00:24:18,742
Patrick.

405
00:24:19,368 --> 00:24:22,746
Patrick, I don't
mean to be self-serving

406
00:24:23,497 --> 00:24:25,540
but I did tell you there
would be another killing.

407
00:24:25,874 --> 00:24:26,959
Yes, you did.

408
00:24:29,044 --> 00:24:30,087
Poor Mullery.

409
00:24:30,587 --> 00:24:32,965
Lisbon was actually on the phone
with him when he died.

410
00:24:34,383 --> 00:24:35,801
Well, I suppose we owe you
an apology, but, uh...

411
00:24:35,926 --> 00:24:37,886
No. No, no, no, no.

412
00:24:38,261 --> 00:24:39,721
Without doubt, there can be no faith.

413
00:24:41,306 --> 00:24:45,185
All I ask is that you'll listen to me now
with, uh, an open mind.

414
00:24:45,310 --> 00:24:47,521
Now that you know
what I say is true.

415
00:24:48,563 --> 00:24:50,691
Perhaps you could imagine
how helpful that could be to you.

416
00:24:53,151 --> 00:24:54,194
Can you imagine that?

417
00:24:57,656 --> 00:25:00,033
- Are you trying to hypnotize me?
- No.

418
00:25:01,284 --> 00:25:02,369
Patrick, of course not.

419
00:25:03,495 --> 00:25:05,247
- Felt a little weird.
- Yeah. No.

420
00:25:06,164 --> 00:25:07,207
Trying is nothing.

421
00:25:07,332 --> 00:25:11,962
Doing is everything, and all I'm doing
is asking for you to let me help you.

422
00:25:13,630 --> 00:25:14,673
I can help you.

423
00:25:18,385 --> 00:25:20,178
You can help us. Come on in.

424
00:25:20,637 --> 00:25:21,680
Thank you.

425
00:25:25,183 --> 00:25:27,185
Lisbon, Sheriff, you remember Mr. Mars?

426
00:25:27,686 --> 00:25:29,479
- Yes.
- He can help.

427
00:25:30,105 --> 00:25:31,189
- Really?
- He can?

428
00:25:31,606 --> 00:25:33,692
Well, I know you're skeptical,
but please, hear him out.

429
00:25:33,817 --> 00:25:35,444
I think you'll be very impressed.

430
00:25:35,652 --> 00:25:36,695
I need to run to the store.

431
00:25:36,820 --> 00:25:38,947
Then we should take him out to
Keeley's place to look around.

432
00:25:39,281 --> 00:25:40,323
I'll be right back.

433
00:25:43,869 --> 00:25:45,954
- I sense that you're upset.
- Oh, really?

434
00:25:46,788 --> 00:25:48,373
Oh, yes, I see.

435
00:25:49,624 --> 00:25:53,003
You were the last person to speak
with Sheriff Mullery, weren't you?

436
00:25:53,295 --> 00:25:54,337
Yes.

437
00:25:54,713 --> 00:25:56,840
Oh, I see you right there
when he died.

438
00:25:58,800 --> 00:25:59,968
No. No.

439
00:26:01,511 --> 00:26:03,096
Right there, but not there.

440
00:26:05,849 --> 00:26:07,684
You were on the phone with him
when he died.

441
00:26:09,811 --> 00:26:10,854
How did you know that?

442
00:26:13,607 --> 00:26:14,649
A psychic?

443
00:26:17,986 --> 00:26:19,029
You're kidding me.

444
00:26:22,741 --> 00:26:25,077
- Come on.
- This guy's good.

445
00:26:45,222 --> 00:26:47,849
- What room is that?
- It's the bathroom.

446
00:26:49,810 --> 00:26:51,019
So what-what's the gag?

447
00:26:51,144 --> 00:26:53,396
Because this appears to be
an absurd waste of time.

448
00:26:53,522 --> 00:26:54,689
Yes, it does, doesn't it?

449
00:26:55,107 --> 00:26:57,317
Wait a minute, though.
You'll enjoy the next part.

450
00:26:58,068 --> 00:27:00,862
The mark of a good psychic
is the ability to improvise

451
00:27:00,987 --> 00:27:05,909
to use to your advantage whatever
props might come to hand.

452
00:27:13,291 --> 00:27:14,417
What is it? A tooth?

453
00:27:15,961 --> 00:27:17,003
A wolf's tooth.

454
00:27:17,129 --> 00:27:19,131
Where on earth would
a wolf's tooth come from?

455
00:27:21,883 --> 00:27:23,426
The killer must have left it here.

456
00:27:25,428 --> 00:27:26,471
It's a sign.

457
00:27:28,807 --> 00:27:32,435
As a clairvoyant, a man
of clear sight, if you will

458
00:27:33,186 --> 00:27:36,481
it's my opinion the killer has a
strong lycanthropic complex.

459
00:27:36,606 --> 00:27:39,109
He believes he's a
werewolf or a wolf man...

460
00:27:39,776 --> 00:27:41,695
"were" being an
anglo-saxon word for "man."

461
00:27:41,820 --> 00:27:44,239
Hence, of course, the monthly
pattern of his crimes.

462
00:27:44,364 --> 00:27:46,825
His behavior is driven
by the phases of the moon.

463
00:27:46,950 --> 00:27:49,327
You're saying there's a killer out there
that turns into a wolf?

464
00:27:49,911 --> 00:27:50,954
No, no, no, no.

465
00:27:51,079 --> 00:27:54,249
No, I'm saying there's a killer out there
who believes he turns into a wolf

466
00:27:54,374 --> 00:27:57,544
and so he is a wolf
and behaves like one.

467
00:27:57,669 --> 00:28:00,005
The mind is a powerful machine.

468
00:28:00,130 --> 00:28:03,383
It can create reality.
And that is all...

469
00:28:03,508 --> 00:28:05,385
Now I think I know
what your game is.

470
00:28:05,802 --> 00:28:06,845
Really?

471
00:28:07,053 --> 00:28:08,847
I'd be very surprised if you did.

472
00:28:11,641 --> 00:28:13,351
Of course. You're right.

473
00:28:13,602 --> 00:28:15,312
The killer is driven
by the phases of the moon.

474
00:28:15,437 --> 00:28:17,022
That is brilliant.
How did you work that out?

475
00:28:17,898 --> 00:28:18,940
Simple deduction.

476
00:28:19,274 --> 00:28:21,109
And the last killing didn't break
the pattern at all.

477
00:28:21,234 --> 00:28:23,320
It simply initiated a
different lunar cycle.

478
00:28:24,487 --> 00:28:27,115
- Exactly.
- So tonight... it's a big night, huh?

479
00:28:27,908 --> 00:28:29,534
- Tonight?
- Moon's at its apogee.

480
00:28:30,702 --> 00:28:33,288
I'm no astrologer, but, uh, isn't
that a highly charged moment?

481
00:28:33,580 --> 00:28:35,165
It can be. It depends.

482
00:28:35,290 --> 00:28:38,084
And since serial killers always
have a very strong urge

483
00:28:38,210 --> 00:28:40,212
to return to the scene
of their crimes...

484
00:28:41,796 --> 00:28:43,632
Yeah. Wouldn't wanna be up
in those hills tonight.

485
00:28:43,965 --> 00:28:45,800
That werewolf stuff
gives me the creeps.

486
00:28:46,134 --> 00:28:48,970
Well, no, it's, uh, easy to be scared
of the unknown, hmm?

487
00:28:49,179 --> 00:28:50,972
- Would you excuse me?
- Well, of course.

488
00:28:53,516 --> 00:28:55,227
Mr. Mars, can I get one of those?

489
00:28:57,562 --> 00:28:59,022
- Here, have one of there.
- Thank you.

490
00:28:59,147 --> 00:29:03,235
Sheriff Woolgar, do you have any deputies
watching over the original crime scene?

491
00:29:03,818 --> 00:29:04,861
No. Why?

492
00:29:04,986 --> 00:29:07,155
Well, I've been thinking
about all this wolf stuff

493
00:29:07,280 --> 00:29:08,573
and it occurs to me that

494
00:29:08,698 --> 00:29:11,785
it's very likely that the killer will return
to the scene of the crime.

495
00:29:12,077 --> 00:29:14,704
- Why?
- Well, serial killers do, don't they?

496
00:29:15,538 --> 00:29:17,916
Well, some do and some don't.

497
00:29:18,583 --> 00:29:21,002
Someone that thinks they're a wolf
will almost certainly return.

498
00:29:21,711 --> 00:29:23,630
Wolves always return to their kills.

499
00:29:26,007 --> 00:29:27,050
They do.

500
00:29:27,801 --> 00:29:29,010
I'll put some men up there.

501
00:29:30,553 --> 00:29:31,596
That's smart.

502
00:30:46,212 --> 00:30:47,756
Good evening. Get in.

503
00:30:56,556 --> 00:30:58,516
What on earth are you doing out here?

504
00:31:00,185 --> 00:31:01,478
I was waiting for the killer.

505
00:31:03,229 --> 00:31:05,774
But he saw me.
He chased me.

506
00:31:06,608 --> 00:31:08,360
That wasn't the killer.
That was the police

507
00:31:08,610 --> 00:31:10,320
and now they probably think
you're the killer.

508
00:31:12,322 --> 00:31:14,366
Oh, my God. Oh my...

509
00:31:15,033 --> 00:31:17,202
- This is your fault.
- Excuse me?

510
00:31:17,786 --> 00:31:18,828
How so?

511
00:31:18,953 --> 00:31:20,789
Did I tell you to go out there
in the middle of the night?

512
00:31:20,914 --> 00:31:22,082
No, I did not.

513
00:31:22,540 --> 00:31:24,417
Uh, I'll go to the police.
I'll explain things to them.

514
00:31:24,542 --> 00:31:26,544
They'll see it's a misunderstanding.

515
00:31:26,836 --> 00:31:29,339
Yeah, sure, they will. They'll only keep
you locked up a couple of days.

516
00:31:29,506 --> 00:31:32,300
As long as no angry cops try
to take revenge, you'll be fine.

517
00:31:32,425 --> 00:31:37,055
- Oh, my God. Oh, my God.
- Don't worry. I'll help you.

518
00:31:37,639 --> 00:31:40,058
We can fix this, okay?
Just trust me.

519
00:31:50,358 --> 00:31:52,568
Todd. It's Jane.
Meet me at the Sheriff's office.

520
00:31:56,697 --> 00:32:00,368
Lisbon. Lisbon, get dressed.
I have an excellent play in motion.

521
00:32:02,120 --> 00:32:03,162
Lisbon.

522
00:32:05,665 --> 00:32:06,707
What is it?

523
00:32:07,917 --> 00:32:08,960
Uh, get dressed.

524
00:32:09,502 --> 00:32:10,837
I have an excellent play in motion.

525
00:32:22,807 --> 00:32:25,017
We're about to crack the biggest
case this area's had in a long time

526
00:32:25,143 --> 00:32:26,436
and we are running out of time.

527
00:32:28,980 --> 00:32:31,566
I want him flagged for arrest
at all the airports in the area.

528
00:32:32,358 --> 00:32:33,443
What's happening?
They found someone?

529
00:32:33,943 --> 00:32:35,194
Ask Sheriff Woolgar.

530
00:32:36,529 --> 00:32:38,906
Well, then let me talk to someone
who can, for crying out loud!

531
00:32:40,408 --> 00:32:42,410
- Yes, I'll wait.
- What's the rumpus?

532
00:32:43,369 --> 00:32:45,204
Bob, it's okay. I promise
I won't do anything crazy.

533
00:32:45,329 --> 00:32:46,372
I just wanna know.

534
00:32:47,039 --> 00:32:49,917
My guys spotted Ellis Mars
creeping around the crime scene.

535
00:32:50,418 --> 00:32:52,420
He ran off when challenged, left his car.

536
00:32:52,962 --> 00:32:56,382
We found dried blood and a long,
dark human hair the backseat.

537
00:32:56,674 --> 00:32:57,925
It's on its way to forensics now.

538
00:32:58,050 --> 00:32:59,469
Ellis Mars?
That doesn't make sense.

539
00:32:59,594 --> 00:33:01,179
Oh, we should have realized before.

540
00:33:01,471 --> 00:33:04,223
He knew Lisbon was on the phone
with Mullery when he died.

541
00:33:04,640 --> 00:33:05,892
Psychic powers, he said.

542
00:33:06,017 --> 00:33:07,560
Wait. He knew that?
How could he know that?

543
00:33:07,685 --> 00:33:08,728
Exactly.

544
00:33:09,103 --> 00:33:10,146
He's not psychic.

545
00:33:10,688 --> 00:33:11,731
He was there.

546
00:33:11,856 --> 00:33:13,357
Yeah, but he...
he's been helping you guys.

547
00:33:13,524 --> 00:33:16,569
It's a common psychopathic
profile, classic almost.

548
00:33:16,944 --> 00:33:19,530
They seek a part in the investigation,
they seek the spotlight.

549
00:33:19,697 --> 00:33:21,866
- Well, where is he now?
- We don't know.

550
00:33:23,576 --> 00:33:27,371
Hey. This is acting Sheriff Woolgar,
Vineland township.

551
00:33:27,788 --> 00:33:28,831
Yes.

552
00:33:29,499 --> 00:33:31,292
Count to ten, then meet me outside.

553
00:33:32,585 --> 00:33:35,046
Well, what the heck do I have to do
to get people at the airport?

554
00:33:36,631 --> 00:33:37,673
Yes, now!

555
00:33:49,352 --> 00:33:50,394
Get in.

556
00:33:53,606 --> 00:33:54,899
- Where are we going?
- You'll see.

557
00:33:56,108 --> 00:33:57,151
Get in.

558
00:34:25,846 --> 00:34:27,890
Ellis Mars is in room 21.

559
00:34:28,599 --> 00:34:30,977
Consider it a gift
from a brother in arms.

560
00:34:31,519 --> 00:34:33,646
I hope someday someone
will do the same for me.

561
00:34:43,656 --> 00:34:44,949
You're messing with me.

562
00:34:46,576 --> 00:34:48,828
No. I'll show you.

563
00:35:09,432 --> 00:35:10,474
See?

564
00:35:11,100 --> 00:35:12,977
- Oh, my God.
- Go ahead.

565
00:35:17,148 --> 00:35:18,399
What are you waiting for?

566
00:35:18,858 --> 00:35:20,943
This guy cut Keeley's
throat and watched her die.

567
00:35:21,235 --> 00:35:22,528
I... I didn't.

568
00:35:22,653 --> 00:35:25,906
Don't bother lying to us, Mars. We found
Keeley's blood and hair in your car.

569
00:35:26,324 --> 00:35:27,366
That's impossible!

570
00:35:28,451 --> 00:35:29,702
What's going on, Todd?

571
00:35:32,747 --> 00:35:34,040
I'm not gonna shoot this guy.

572
00:35:35,791 --> 00:35:38,628
All that talk of revenge was just hot
air, was it? You don't have the guts.

573
00:35:38,753 --> 00:35:40,421
Oh, I got the guts, all right.

574
00:35:41,922 --> 00:35:45,635
- I just don't know for sure he did it.
- We have hard evidence against him.

575
00:35:45,760 --> 00:35:48,804
Yes, you do, and
I don't understand that.

576
00:35:49,930 --> 00:35:52,767
I guess you're framing him
for some reason, but why?

577
00:35:54,435 --> 00:35:57,855
And why would you want me to kill
someone who didn't really do it?

578
00:35:58,272 --> 00:35:59,315
What's your game?

579
00:36:01,067 --> 00:36:02,109
Huh?

580
00:36:02,401 --> 00:36:03,736
You're setting me up, aren't you?

581
00:36:06,155 --> 00:36:09,867
Or I'm simply telling you
the truth, and Mars is guilty.

582
00:36:10,451 --> 00:36:11,494
No.

583
00:36:13,162 --> 00:36:14,205
No?

584
00:36:15,539 --> 00:36:16,749
And you're sure?

585
00:36:20,628 --> 00:36:21,837
Yeah, well, you're right.

586
00:36:22,630 --> 00:36:25,633
Mars' only crime is
foolishness and fraud.

587
00:36:26,258 --> 00:36:27,301
I set him up.

588
00:36:28,135 --> 00:36:29,178
Get outta here.

589
00:36:38,020 --> 00:36:39,063
What's this about?

590
00:36:41,399 --> 00:36:42,566
See, the thing is, Todd

591
00:36:44,151 --> 00:36:46,946
there's only one way you can
be so sure of his innocence.

592
00:36:48,364 --> 00:36:52,243
It was you that killed Keeley
and Mullery and all of those cops.

593
00:36:53,703 --> 00:36:54,745
You're nuts.

594
00:36:54,870 --> 00:36:57,289
Oh, don't-don't bother with the song
and dance. I'm satisfied you're guilty.

595
00:36:57,415 --> 00:36:59,083
You're a garden variety psychopath.

596
00:36:59,291 --> 00:37:02,002
Probably been killing random
people here and there for years.

597
00:37:02,378 --> 00:37:05,131
I'm not sure why you turned
on cops all of a sudden

598
00:37:05,256 --> 00:37:06,966
but I'm sure you have
your good reasons.

599
00:37:07,091 --> 00:37:09,927
- Your dad was a cop, right?
- Yes, he was.

600
00:37:10,511 --> 00:37:12,847
He was mean to you, made
you hate policemen, maybe?

601
00:37:12,972 --> 00:37:15,516
No.
He was a kind and gentle man.

602
00:37:15,850 --> 00:37:17,351
Then who was it that
made you this way?

603
00:37:17,476 --> 00:37:18,561
And please don't say
it was your mother.

604
00:37:18,686 --> 00:37:21,772
- That'd be too cliché.
- No. My mother was a saint.

605
00:37:23,566 --> 00:37:24,817
You're mistaken about me.

606
00:37:25,067 --> 00:37:27,069
- Really.
- Really?

607
00:37:29,405 --> 00:37:30,948
Well, let me run it down for you.

608
00:37:31,574 --> 00:37:34,160
You killed Keeley because
she was getting too clingy.

609
00:37:34,410 --> 00:37:36,954
She was asking too many
questions about your other life.

610
00:37:37,079 --> 00:37:38,205
She bugged you.

611
00:37:38,956 --> 00:37:43,252
Mullery died because he recognized that
button as yours, and he asked you about it.

612
00:37:43,377 --> 00:37:46,881
You stalled him, then you
had to kill him quick.

613
00:37:47,423 --> 00:37:50,968
Broke the monthly pattern, which
wasn't about the moon, of course.

614
00:37:51,927 --> 00:37:54,263
Once a month, Hunnicker
got his V.A. check

615
00:37:54,430 --> 00:37:57,725
goes on a binge, drinks till he
blacks out, and gives you an alibi.

616
00:37:57,892 --> 00:37:59,143
How am I doing so far?

617
00:38:01,020 --> 00:38:03,022
- You're very clever, Mr. Jane.
- Thank you.

618
00:38:03,189 --> 00:38:05,399
I must confess, I underestimated you.

619
00:38:05,691 --> 00:38:08,277
More like you
overestimated yourself, Todd.

620
00:38:08,402 --> 00:38:11,781
No. You say that only because you
don't see the whole big picture.

621
00:38:11,906 --> 00:38:15,409
There's a reason for
everything I've done.

622
00:38:16,243 --> 00:38:18,579
- What's the big picture?
- You'll never know.

623
00:38:18,954 --> 00:38:20,998
Right.
There's no big picture.

624
00:38:21,207 --> 00:38:26,712
Oh, trust me.
It would blow... Your... Mind.

625
00:38:31,258 --> 00:38:33,469
No firing pin. Oops.

626
00:38:34,261 --> 00:38:35,304
Of course.

627
00:38:37,389 --> 00:38:38,557
Cho! Rigsby!

628
00:38:39,683 --> 00:38:41,435
- Drop the weapon!
- Hands on your head!

629
00:38:45,773 --> 00:38:46,816
You're under arrest!

630
00:39:13,592 --> 00:39:14,635
What is it?

631
00:39:16,720 --> 00:39:17,763
I've changed my mind.

632
00:39:18,389 --> 00:39:19,431
I wanna talk.

633
00:39:20,599 --> 00:39:21,767
That's a smart move.

634
00:39:22,101 --> 00:39:25,604
- But I'll only talk to Mr. Jane.
- Why is that?

635
00:39:27,106 --> 00:39:28,148
We have a connection.

636
00:39:29,275 --> 00:39:30,693
Maybe he doesn't want to talk to you.

637
00:39:33,404 --> 00:39:34,613
Tell him I have a secret.

638
00:39:36,490 --> 00:39:38,409
A secret only he will understand.

639
00:39:44,498 --> 00:39:45,791
A secret, huh?

640
00:39:45,916 --> 00:39:49,044
That's what he said, that you were
the only one who would understand

641
00:39:49,295 --> 00:39:51,005
that you and he had a connection.

642
00:39:51,672 --> 00:39:55,426
He's just trying to lend an air of mystery
to his sad little story of madness.

643
00:39:57,136 --> 00:39:59,972
The case will go a lot easier
if we can get him to talk.

644
00:40:00,180 --> 00:40:02,850
I have spent enough time
with that creep.

645
00:40:03,100 --> 00:40:05,853
Staring into the abyss.
You know, it's not healthy.

646
00:40:06,145 --> 00:40:09,023
Please?
Just hear what he has to say.

647
00:40:09,481 --> 00:40:10,941
He's right downstairs
in the holding cell.

648
00:40:57,154 --> 00:40:59,073
P.S.U. is starting an investigation.

649
00:40:59,698 --> 00:41:02,493
They figure it's somebody getting
revenge for officer Howard.

650
00:41:04,203 --> 00:41:05,746
They'll need to speak with you
later today.

651
00:41:06,622 --> 00:41:07,665
Okay.

652
00:41:09,208 --> 00:41:11,377
Doctor says his systems
are shutting down

653
00:41:12,002 --> 00:41:13,712
that he won't regain consciousness.

654
00:41:21,553 --> 00:41:22,596
Okay, then.

655
00:41:22,888 --> 00:41:24,598
I'm gonna go finish up
the paperwork now.

656
00:41:46,537 --> 00:41:48,747
"Tyger, tyger..."

